00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.236
This is Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. It's the foundation for building
agents that you can trust and scale at enterprise level.

00:05:02.876 --> 00:05:05.476
There are seven services. One foundation.

00:05:05.876 --> 00:05:09.316
Everything you need to run agents in production, right.

00:05:09.796 --> 00:05:13.996
So you have runtime to execute.
You have memory to give them the context.

00:05:14.596 --> 00:05:18.076
You have identity that gives you the accountability for an agent.

00:05:18.076 --> 00:05:21.236
And then you have gateway that connects them to your tools.

00:05:21.236 --> 00:05:26.916
The code interpreter and browser allows you allows agent
to act on behalf of you within the system.

00:05:27.556 --> 00:05:30.516
And you have an observability which is overseeing.

00:05:30.516 --> 00:05:33.636
So you always know what they did and why they did that.

00:05:34.156 --> 00:05:36.996
And every one of these was built for a single purpose.

00:05:37.156 --> 00:05:40.076
So you can trust your agents in production from day one.

00:05:41.796 --> 00:05:47.036
But all of this, right? Every service,
every pillar means nothing without one thing,

00:05:48.516 --> 00:05:53.956
which is data, right? The truth is,
an agent is only as good as the data it runs on.

00:05:54.716 --> 00:05:56.916
You have wrong data. You have wrong action.

00:05:57.276 --> 00:06:03.036
And that's why every real agent story doesn't begin with a model,
it actually begins with the data.

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:07.836
And that's exactly where the journey
at the Ministry of Education began.

00:06:08.436 --> 00:06:15.036
Please welcome the deputy CIO of Ministry of Education,
Miss Karen Chiah, to share their story where it all started with data.

00:06:18.276 --> 00:06:19.036
Thank you.

00:06:19.916 --> 00:06:20.796
Thank you.

00:06:23.956 --> 00:06:27.996
Good afternoon everyone. It is an honor to be here at the AWS summit.

00:06:28.636 --> 00:06:32.396
Today, I'll share how the Ministry of Education is harnessing AI

00:06:32.796 --> 00:06:38.556
and cloud technologies to drive operational excellence
in Singapore's education system.

00:06:41.636 --> 00:06:48.316
At MoE, our division is known
as DxD Digital Excellence and Products Division.

00:06:49.276 --> 00:06:52.756
We see ourselves as digital leaders and agile innovators.

00:06:53.476 --> 00:07:01.636
We embrace, change, experiment boldly and learn continuously
to stay ahead in a rapidly evolving landscape.

00:07:02.796 --> 00:07:10.316
We aim to solve real problems and transform experiences not just
for efficiency, but for meaningful impact.

00:07:11.436 --> 00:07:17.996
We believe that these are the qualities that will position us
to lead the transformation in education technology.

00:07:21.116 --> 00:07:29.156
Our ecosystem is vast. 500,000 students,
36,000 educators, and 350 schools.

00:07:30.036 --> 00:07:34.276
Add to that 400,000 parents and 46,000 staff.

00:07:35.396 --> 00:07:42.116
Each group has unique needs from learning effectively
to accessing information seamlessly.

00:07:43.196 --> 00:07:51.516
Our systems have to cater to all of them at scale,
and technology enables us to meet these diverse demands.

00:07:54.236 --> 00:08:01.156
Before I continue further, let me show a short video to better
illustrate the reality faced by the team in our work.

00:08:03.076 --> 00:08:11.756
Meet Mr. Wong Engineering Manager at Singapore's Ministry
of Education, responsible for 130 AWS accounts.

00:08:12.476 --> 00:08:17.836
Every day, his team drowns in consoles,
dashboards that don't talk to each other.

00:08:17.916 --> 00:08:26.396
Everyone looking at a different screen, 130 accounts,
each with its own costs, its own alerts, its own security posture.

00:08:26.396 --> 00:08:36.156
No single view weeks consumed by manual work, copying numbers into
spreadsheets, rebuilding the same report from scratch every month.

00:08:36.756 --> 00:08:39.916
Leadership needs answers, real time answers.

00:08:40.396 --> 00:08:44.476
But the data isn't there. Every screen tells a different story.

00:08:44.676 --> 00:08:46.796
None of them tells the full picture.

00:08:47.156 --> 00:08:51.036
Security alerts. Fire in the dark. Late nights become routine.

00:08:51.396 --> 00:09:00.596
There is no automation, no relief. One evening, staring at a
whiteboard covered in broken connections,

00:09:00.956 --> 00:09:02.596
Mr. Wong made a decision, this had to change.

00:09:07.356 --> 00:09:15.036
As our systems grow in complexity across microservices,
cloud infrastructure, and third party integrations.

00:09:15.556 --> 00:09:25.076
The silo approach caused inefficiencies, limits centralized oversight,
and hinders teamwork, decision making, and optimization.

00:09:25.956 --> 00:09:31.756
Rising cloud costs and emerging security
threats demands for innovative solutions.

00:09:32.396 --> 00:09:39.436
Our focus is to simplify operations and enhance
security while meeting these growing demands.

00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:45.836
So how did we overcome these challenges?

00:09:46.596 --> 00:09:52.556
We knew we couldn't do this alone.
Creating the right partnership was absolutely critical.

00:09:52.996 --> 00:09:58.156
And I want to emphasize these were true
partnerships, not vendor relationships.

00:09:58.996 --> 00:10:00.000
We partnered with AWS for two things the infrastructure
to run this reliably for millions of interactions,

